@startuml run_target_transition

title Run Target Transition

box "Caller thread" #FAFAF0
participant "State Manager" as Client
end box

queue "Component Event Queue" as EventQueue

box "Main loop thread" #F0F4FA
participant "ProcessGroupManager" as PGM
participant "Graph" as Graph
participant "Transition" as Transition
end box

queue "Worker Queue" as WorkerQueue

note over EventQueue
  Requests from outside and results from the workers travel
  through the same queue, which is what serializes them into
  the main loop.
end note

' --- request -----------------------------------------------------------------
Client -> EventQueue: request a Run Target
EventQueue -> PGM ++: run target request

alt #Pink the Run Target does not exist, or it is already active or being transitioned to
    PGM --> Client: request rejected

else #White a transition to another Run Target is running
    PGM -> Graph ++: set new pending Run Target
    PGM -> Graph: cancel the running transition
    note over Graph
      kInTransition becomes kAborting and the stop token of the
      running transition is requested.
    end note
    deactivate Graph
    PGM --> Client: request accepted

else #White the graph is not in a transition
    PGM -> Graph ++: set new pending Run Target
    deactivate Graph
    PGM --> Client: request accepted
end
deactivate PGM

note over PGM
  The caller is only told whether the request was accepted. It is
  not blocked until the Run Target is reached.
end note

' --- start -------------------------------------------------------------------
PGM -> PGM ++: handle the graph state
note over PGM
  A pending Run Target is only picked up while the graph is in
  kSuccess or kUndefinedState, so a cancelled transition is first
  allowed to settle.
end note

PGM -> Graph ++: start the transition
note over Graph
  The graph moves to kInTransition
end note

Graph -> Transition ++: set up a transition to the Run Target
note over Transition
  The transition works in two phases:
  1. Stopping phase: every component that is running and is not
  needed by the target has to be stopped. This includes components left
  running by an aborted transition before.
  2. Starting phase: every component that is deactivated but is needed
  by the target has to be activated.
end note

' --- dispatch ----------------------------------------------------------------
loop while the transition reports components that are ready to be started/stopped
    Transition --> Graph: a component is ready to be activated/stopped

    alt #White the component is a Run Target node
        Graph -> Graph: activate or deactivate in place
        Graph -> Transition: component has finished
        note over Graph
          Run Target nodes are virtual and have no process, so
          they finish immediately and can make further components
          ready within the same dispatch loop.
        end note

    else #White the component is a process
        Graph -> WorkerQueue: activation or deactivation of the component
        note over WorkerQueue
          What a worker does with it is shown in Component
          Activation and Component Deactivation.
        end note
    end
end
deactivate Transition
deactivate Graph
deactivate PGM
note over Graph
  Only components without unfinished predecessors are dispatched;
  the rest follow as their predecessors report back. Several
  components are therefore in flight at the same time.
end note

' --- progress ----------------------------------------------------------------
loop one iteration per component result
    EventQueue -> PGM ++: component event
    PGM -> Graph ++: apply the component event

    alt #Pink the component failed
        note over Graph
          The graph moves to kAborting and the stop token is
          requested: components that have not been taken by a
          worker report as skipped, components in flight finish
          their current step - see Transition Cancellation /
          Preemption.
        end note

    else #White the component finished and the phase is not complete
        Graph -> Transition ++: component has finished
        Transition --> Graph --: the components this one has unblocked
        Graph -> WorkerQueue: activation or deactivation of those components
        note over Transition
          Activation: a component is ready once all of its
          dependencies are active. Deactivation: once all of its
          dependents are stopped.
        end note

    else #White the stopping phase is complete
        Graph -> Transition ++: component has finished
        note over Transition
          Starting phase: the components of the target subgraph
          that are not active yet, starting with those that have
          no dependencies left to wait for.
        end note
        Transition --> Graph --: the components ready to be started
        Graph -> WorkerQueue: activation of those components

    else #F2FAF2 the starting phase is complete
        Graph -> Transition ++: component has finished
        Transition --> Graph --: the transition is finished
        note over Graph
          The graph moves to kSuccess: the requested Run Target
          is the active one.
        end note
        Graph -> Client: the Run Target is active
    end

    deactivate Graph
    deactivate PGM
end

note over Graph
  An aborted transition ends differently: once the last component
  in flight has reported back, the graph moves to kUndefinedState
  and the main loop starts a transition to the fallback Run Target
  - see Process Termination and Supervision Failure.
end note

legend bottom
  The graph never waits for a component. It dispatches what is ready, returns to
  the main loop, and advances the transition whenever a result arrives.

  Only processes are given to the workers; Run Target nodes are resolved on the
  main loop thread.
end legend

@enduml
